## Part 4: MVP Recommendations for ONAY (Weeks–1 Month)

ONAY’s distinctive architecture — **shared station + pre-built voice segment library + AI assembly pipeline + provider-agnostic timeline manifests** — gives it a structural cost and stability advantage over both competitors. Per-listener real-time TTS (Yoodio) is expensive and latency-prone; a rigid single-host script-only approach (Radiant) breaks when its scraping/integration backend hiccups, producing the visible “Emergency Fix” releases. ONAY’s pre-built segments + assembly pipeline means commentary is deterministic, cacheable, CDN-deliverable, and doesn’t degrade if Claude/Replicate is down for an hour. Lean into that.

### MVP-1. Ship one host extremely well, not three “mid”

- **WHY:** Yoodio surfaces 3 named hosts but reviewers still say “only 3 to choose from” — meaning the personality differentiation is not sticking. Radiant succeeded with one host (Rad) by giving him a strong, opinionated voice (“C3PO not HAL,” sarcastic, robot-as-feature). A solo dev cannot author convincing personalities for 3+ hosts at launch.
- **WHAT:** A single named ONAY host with a tight, written-down voice bible (3–5 adjectives, sample lines, taboo topics, signature phrases, response to tragedy, response to a banger). Use the male/female toggle pattern Radiant validated, but ship one canonical voice first.
- **HOW:** In Segment Studio, encode the voice bible as the system prompt baseline for Claude Sonnet, and lock 8–12 reference clips for Chatterbox voice cloning so every segment is sonically consistent. Keep one Replicate Chatterbox voice fingerprint; pre-render all evergreen segments (greetings, transitions, sign-offs, “more music coming up”) and cache them on object storage. Only generate truly dynamic segments (per-track intros) on-demand.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** Radiant’s biggest praise is voice character (“they want C3PO not HAL”); its biggest pain is brittle integrations. ONAY’s pre-built segment library means the voice can stay consistent forever — re-running the same Claude prompt with a new model later won’t drift the personality because most segments are already in the library.

### MVP-2. Provider-agnostic timeline = your wedge — but launch with one provider

- **WHY:** Radiant burned years on Spotify approval, got killed commercially, and rebuilt on Apple Music — losing market share. Yoodio still appears Spotify-anchored despite a MacRumors Apple Music thread. Both companies are paying a *huge* tax for trying to bridge providers.
- **WHAT:** Lead with **Apple Music** (no commercial-approval gauntlet, MusicKit handles auth + DRM playback, larger paying audience for indie dev distribution), keeping the Spotify timeline manifest implementation behind a feature flag for Phase 2.
- **HOW:** Your timeline manifest abstraction is already provider-agnostic — pick Apple Music’s `ApplicationMusicPlayer` for shipping v1 because: (a) MusicKit’s `MusicSubscription.canPlayCatalogContent` gives a clean gating signal, (b) MetaBrainz + Apple Music catalog lookup gives you the metadata Radiant uses (BPM, valence, era), and (c) you avoid Spotify’s 25-user developer-quota cap that throttled Yoodio’s beta. Reserve a “Coming Soon: Spotify” pill in onboarding so Spotify users sign up and you build a waitlist.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** Spotify’s 2023 AI DJ launch and 2025 conversational expansion mean Spotify users already have an in-app DJ. Apple Music users don’t — that’s the audience Radiant has been quietly serving for two years and the audience YouTube Music’s “Beyond the Beat” doesn’t touch.

### MVP-3. Three commentary types only — but make them feel premium

- **WHY:** Yoodio’s 5-type commentary library (deep dives, life stories, weather/forecast, lyric breakdowns, local-business shoutouts) is broad but shallow. Radiant ships news + weather + traffic + artist back-stories + concert recs but breaks frequently. Doing fewer types reliably beats doing many types badly — and the “mismatched track playback” issue at Radiant happens because each integration is a failure surface.
- **WHAT:** Ship exactly three commentary types in v1:
1. **Track intros / outros** (artist context, “this song came out in X year”). These are the table stakes both apps deliver.
1. **Weather, geographic, and time-of-day awareness** (one segment per session, not per track). This is what makes the experience feel “live.”
1. **Station-themed micro-essays** (one per station type — e.g., for a “late-night drive” station, a 15-second mood-setter). This is what neither competitor does well.
- **HOW:** Pre-build artist back-story segments lazily — when an Apple Music track first appears in any user’s queue, generate the Claude script, render it via Chatterbox on Replicate, and cache by `(artist_id, host_voice_id)`. After the first listener triggers it, all subsequent listeners get the cached audio for ~$0.003 amortized to near-zero. Weather and time-of-day segments can use a small set of templated, pre-rendered openers per (city × weather × time bucket) combination — at ~50 cities × 6 weather states × 4 time buckets, that’s ~1,200 pre-rendered clips, costing **under $5 total** at Replicate’s $0.003/run rate.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** This is where ONAY’s shared-station architecture *crushes* Yoodio and matches Radiant. Yoodio re-generates per-listener; ONAY shares. Radiant’s news scraping breaks; ONAY’s weather is a clean OpenWeatherMap call into a pre-rendered template. Defer real news (Radiant’s brittlest dependency) to Phase 2 entirely.

### MVP-4. News/weather/traffic — minimal scope

- **WHY:** Radiant’s most-praised feature is “you’ll learn… how much traffic you can expect on your commute” ([App Store description](https://apps.apple.com/ca/app/radiant-the-future-of-radio/id1476163061)), but it’s also the most-broken. International users want local newspapers and language-matched voices; Radiant defaults to US sources and an American English accent regardless ([AppleVis](https://www.applevis.com/apps/ios/music/radiant-future-radio)).
- **WHAT:** v1 ships **weather only**, opt-in by city. No news. No traffic. No local-business shoutouts (Yoodio’s most spam-prone segment type per the country-radio complaint).
- **HOW:** OpenWeatherMap free tier; one weather segment near the start of each session, optionally one mid-session if temperature/precipitation crosses a threshold. Use the templated pre-render approach above. If a user grants location, fall through; if they don’t, ask for a default city in onboarding.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** Quinn’s Music Ally interview describes news/weather/traffic as Rad’s killer combo, and his Kill the DJ piece reveals the underlying engineering pain. Don’t take on that engineering surface area as a solo dev. The narrative win — “ONAY actually works” — is more valuable than feature parity.

### MVP-5. Onboarding — match Yoodio’s 2-screen flow but front-load the “aha”

- **WHY:** Yoodio compressed onboarding from 4 to 2 screens ([App Store version history](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/yoodio-radio/id6743950965)). Headspace, Calm, and Spotify all show that asking 3 quick personalization questions and immediately playing something the user already loves drives retention ([VWO mobile onboarding analysis](https://vwo.com/blog/mobile-app-onboarding-guide/); [Music Ally’s account of Radiant doing exactly this](https://musically.com/2020/05/19/startup-files-radiants-spotify-powered-ai-dj/) — Rad opens with a song he knows you love).
- **WHAT:** Three screens max:
1. Sign in with Apple + Apple Music auth (one screen, two actions).
1. Pick 3 artists OR 3 vibes (chips, tap to select).
1. Allow notifications + (optional) location for weather; defer to a sheet later if declined.
   Then immediately drop into a station with the host saying “Welcome to ONAY — playing this because you love [Artist].”
- **HOW:** This is pure SwiftUI work plus one MusicKit auth flow. Borrow the Spotify “pick 3 artists” pattern explicitly; Radiant’s “What are you into?” stage breaks for users ([Google Play](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=co.broadcastapp.Radiant)) — guard your equivalent with timeout fallbacks and a “Skip for now” escape.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** Spotify’s onboarding is the gold standard ([VWO](https://vwo.com/blog/mobile-app-onboarding-guide/)). Yoodio’s two-screen flow is a defensible minimum. Radiant’s onboarding is a counter-example — don’t put a critical hard-to-debug API call (artist search) in the only path forward.

### MVP-6. Visual / UX patterns to borrow

- **WHY:** Liquid Glass adoption has become a near-requirement for not-looking-dated on iOS 26+ ([InspiringApps](https://www.inspiringapps.com/blog/ios-18-lessons-preparing-ios-19-app-development); [Apple’s design gallery](https://developer.apple.com/design/new-design-gallery/)). Yoodio adopted it in Oct 2025 ([App Store version history](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/yoodio-radio/id6743950965)); Apple’s marketing keeps spotlighting apps that have. ONAY launching without it would feel like a 2024 app.
- **WHAT:** Adopt Liquid Glass throughout, with two specific patterns from Yoodio worth copying directly: (1) **mesh-gradient station covers** rather than AI-generated artwork (cheaper, never embarrassing, more consistent — Yoodio explicitly retreated to mesh gradients in 1.03.2 after gen-image issues); (2) a **persistent now-playing strip** that doesn’t reset on station change, plus a tab-index indicator that auto-hides.
- **HOW:** Recompile against the iOS 26 SDK and use SwiftUI’s `liquidGlassMaterial` for navigation, tab bars, and the now-playing sheet. Generate mesh gradients procedurally from a 2–3 color palette derived from the station prompt (free, deterministic, looks intentional).
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** Radiant’s UI is unremarkable per Product Hunt screenshots; Yoodio has invested here. Matching Yoodio’s visual polish while shipping fewer broken features is achievable solo.

### MVP-7. Social/sharing — one feature only

- **WHY:** Yoodio added “station sharing functionality” in 1.03.6 ([App Store version history](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/yoodio-radio/id6743950965)) and that’s it for social. Radiant has none. Both are leaving virality on the table, but full social is a Phase-2 distraction.
- **WHAT:** One feature: **shareable station deep-links**. Generate `onay.app/s/<station_id>` URLs that open the app to that exact station; if the recipient doesn’t have ONAY, the link goes to a marketing landing page with a 15-second host-narrated preview clip and an App Store CTA.
- **HOW:** Universal Links + a tiny static landing page. The 15-second preview is just the cached intro segment for that station — already exists in your assembly pipeline.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** Yoodio’s station sharing is a feature; *previewing without install* is a growth channel. This is the ONAY shared-station architecture’s free lunch — Yoodio re-generates per listener and can’t easily preview-without-install; ONAY’s pre-built segments make this trivial.

### MVP-8. Monetization at launch

- **WHY:** Yoodio Pro at $0.99/mo with **$9.99/year** is priced as a tip jar, not a business — and the developer dropped from $2.99/mo to $0.99/mo, suggesting low conversion. Radiant has been free for years with planned-but-undelivered Rad.FM Plus. Both apps require a paid Apple Music subscription anyway, so the user is already paying for music.
- **WHAT:** Free at launch with a single, clearly-labeled **ONAY+ at ~$3.99/month or $29.99/year**, gating: (1) extra hosts/voices once you ship them, (2) faster station generation (priority queue), (3) longer segments, (4) “skip back / replay” on host segments. **Do not** gate core listening — gate convenience and personality variety. Quinn’s Apple Music move freed Radiant from Spotify’s commercial-approval problem; you have the same freedom.
- **HOW:** StoreKit 2, single subscription product. No tiers at launch. Offer a 7-day free trial.
- **COMPETITIVE INSIGHT:** $0.99/mo trains users to think AI radio is worth nothing. Spotify’s AI DJ is gated behind Premium ($10.99+); price ONAY+ closer to $3–4 to signal value, with the Apple Music subscription understood as a prerequisite. Yoodio’s pricing is a cautionary tale, not a target.

