## Part 6: Specific Calls-Outs and Caveats for a Solo Developer

- **Don’t take on real-time news in v1.** Quinn’s Kill the DJ interview is a confession: news/traffic integrations are the brittle part. Defer to month 6+.
- **Don’t replicate Yoodio’s “1,000+ personalities.”** It’s almost certainly procedural recombination, and reviewers don’t believe it. Three actor-licensed hosts beat 1,000 procedural ones.
- **Don’t ship without Liquid Glass.** Apple’s iOS 27 timeline makes adoption effectively mandatory ([InspiringApps](https://www.inspiringapps.com/blog/ios-18-lessons-preparing-ios-19-app-development)). It’s also a one-week SwiftUI port with the iOS 26 SDK.
- **Don’t underprice.** Yoodio at $9.99/year is a cautionary anchor, not an aspiration. $3.99/month gives you a real revenue line.
- **Spotify support is a Phase-2 trap.** Quinn lost years to Spotify’s commercial approval process; Yoodio’s beta was capped at 25 users by Spotify’s dev quota. Apple Music is the path of least resistance for a solo dev.
- **The split-model AI stack you’re planning is correct.** Claude Sonnet for scripts (highest quality writing), Gemini Flash or Grok for structured tasks (cheap, fast entity extraction), Ollama local for fallback (no cloud dependency for testing) — this matches what Radiant evolved to (ChatGPT 3.5 + self-hosted Llama 2 for cost) and is the textbook indie-dev configuration.
- **Replicate Chatterbox costs are negligible compared to Claude.** At $0.003/run × ~300 chars/run, even 1 million segments cost $3,000. Your real cost driver will be Claude API usage on dynamic scripts; segment caching is the antidote.
- **The pre-built segment library is your structural moat.** Reframe internally: ONAY isn’t building “an AI radio host,” it’s building “the world’s largest licensed library of AI-rendered radio segments, with a dynamic assembly engine on top.” That framing makes voice actor licensing, segment caching, and shared stations all part of one coherent strategy that neither Yoodio (per-user generative) nor Radiant (single-host real-time) can copy without rebuilding from scratch.

The single most actionable insight from this research: **ship reliability and stability as a feature.** Both incumbents wear visible reliability scars, and the underlying reason — real-time per-listener generation in Yoodio’s case, brittle third-party scraping in Radiant’s case — is exactly what ONAY’s pre-built-segment + shared-station architecture solves. Lead with that in your App Store copy, and you take the highest-leverage piece of competitive ground available.